// prism_ui_grid -- the data grid (prism/ui/ composition layer, milestone MB.8). INCREMENT 1 of a
// component `prism/ui/README.md` itself sizes at 3-5 person-months ("AG Grid is a ~1 MB commercial
// product... the single biggest component in the whole matrix"). This file does NOT attempt that
// whole surface -- see the explicit scope list below.
//
// ── SCOPE OF INCREMENT 1 (built here) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//   - Column pinning/freezing (a closed 3-state enum per column: start / none / end), with a
//     deterministic, total ordering rule (see below).
//   - Column resize state (`prism_grid_resize_column`) -- a column's width can change after
//     construction, non-mutating, same discipline as reorder.
//   - Row selection: none / single / multi, as a closed enum mode.
//   - Cell-edit state: which (row key, column key) cell is being edited, typed as an Option.
//   - Column reordering (`prism_grid_reorder`), pin-group-aware, non-mutating.
//
// ── EXPLICITLY OUT OF SCOPE FOR THIS INCREMENT (future increments, NOT built here) ────────────
//   - Virtualization (windowed rendering of only the visible row/column range). This file renders
//     every row through `prism_structure.prism_grid` every time -- fine for hundreds of rows, not
//     for AG-Grid-scale (100k+ row) datasets.
//   - Grouping (collapsing rows under a shared key into an expandable tree).
//   - Aggregation (column-level sum/avg/count footers, per-group subtotals).
//   - Fill-down (drag-to-repeat a cell's value across a range).
//   - Clipboard (copy/paste a cell or rectangular range to/from the OS clipboard).
//   - Infinite scroll (incremental backend-driven row loading as the viewport nears the end).
//   - Committing an edited VALUE back into row data. `prism_grid_begin_edit`/`_end_edit` track
//     WHICH cell is being edited (the state a caller's editor UI needs to render an input in the
//     right place) -- they do not write a new value into `rows`, which is a caller-side (and,
//     for undo/redo and validation, a future-increment) concern, adjacent to fill-down/clipboard.
//   - A deselect/toggle-off/clear-selection API. Only `prism_grid_select_row` exists (replace in
//     `single`, accumulate in `multi`, reject in `none` -- exactly PRISM's MB.8 increment-1 spec).
//     A real caller needs a way to unselect eventually; that is deliberately left for the next
//     increment rather than guessed at here, to keep this increment's surface exactly matched to
//     its written spec (and its rejection-case KAT) instead of quietly growing it.
//
// ── GRID vs TABLE (`prism_ui_table.nova`, MB.1) -- WHEN TO USE WHICH ──────────────────────────
// `PrismTable` is a read-mostly, SORTABLE list: one active sort column/direction, no per-cell
// interaction state. `PrismGrid` is an EDITABLE, INTERACTIVE surface: per-column pin/width,
// per-row selection, a single actively-edited cell. Concretely: reach for `prism_table` when the
// UI need is "show sortable rows" (a report, a read-only listing); reach for `prism_grid` when the
// UI need is "let the user select rows, resize/pin/reorder columns, and edit cells" (a data-entry
// surface, a spreadsheet-like view). Both are compositions over the SAME `prism_structure.prism_grid`
// primitive for their body -- `prism_ui_table.nova`'s own header already argues at length for why
// that primitive is the right body for tabular data (mandatory row-identity `by`, so this file does
// not re-argue it) -- and this file reuses that argument rather than duplicating it. What grid adds
// on top that table deliberately does NOT have: per-column pin/width/editable metadata, a selection
// set, and edit-target state -- none of which a plain sortable table needs.
//
// ── WHY PIN STATE IS A PER-COLUMN FIELD, AND WHAT "REJECT MORE PINNED THAN TOTAL" MEANS ──────
// The task spec lists a column's properties as "key, header, extractor, a pin state (closed enum:
// start/none/end) and a width" -- i.e. pin is data carried on EACH column definition, exactly
// alongside key/header/width, not a separate list of pinned keys passed to the grid constructor.
// Given that, the literal condition "more pinned columns than total columns" can never actually
// fire from the per-column-enum encoding: each column contributes to exactly one of
// {start, none, end}, so count(start) + count(end) <= total always, with equality only when
// count(none) == 0. Rather than ship a rejection branch that can never execute (dead code
// masquerading as a validated invariant -- exactly the kind of "looks checked, isn't" gap
// CLAUDE.md's failure-hunt discipline exists to catch), this file treats the equality case as the
// intended, reachable meaning: **at least one column must remain unpinned**. A grid where every
// column is frozen has no scrollable body left -- pinning has lost its meaning (there is nothing
// left to pin AGAINST) -- so `count(start) + count(end) >= total` is rejected at construction with
// a message naming the exact counts. This turns an ambiguous spec sentence into a concrete,
// testable, always-reachable rule (the KAT proves both the boundary that passes -- 3 of 5 pinned --
// and the one that is rejected -- 2 of 2 pinned).
//
// ── PINNED ORDERING: DETERMINISTIC AND TOTAL, VIA A CONTIGUOUS-RANGE INVARIANT ────────────────
// "Start-pinned first in their declared order, then unpinned, then end-pinned" is computed ONCE,
// at construction (`_prism_grid_canonical_order`), by partitioning the caller's column list into
// three buckets in a single pass (each bucket preserving the RELATIVE order columns already had in
// the input -- the same stable-partition technique `prism_ui_table.nova`'s descending-sort helper
// uses for tied keys) and concatenating start ++ mid ++ end. The result is stored as `PrismGrid`'s
// OWN column order -- there is no separate "declared order" vs "display order" to keep in sync.
// This is what makes `prism_grid_reorder`'s pin-group guard cheap and provably correct: because
// the stored order is ALWAYS start-block-then-mid-block-then-end-block, each group occupies a
// CONTIGUOUS index range (`[0, count_start)`, `[count_start, n-count_end)`, `[n-count_end, n)`),
// so "would this move cross a pin group" reduces to one range-containment check
// (`_prism_grid_pin_range`) instead of re-scanning neighbours or re-deriving groups per call.
//
// ── FIELD-NAME COLLISIONS: WHY EVERY FIELD HERE IS PREFIXED ───────────────────────────────────
// NOVA's struct-field resolution is by NAME, not by (declaring type, name) -- confirmed as a live
// defect, not a style nit: it crashed the HTML renderer on 2026-08-16 when `PrismFormField.kind`
// (registered later) silently stole `PrismNode.kind`'s slot, so `PrismNode.kind` read back as an
// unrelated field's value on a mixed import. The fix in THIS file is prevention, not cure: every
// field on every type declared below carries a component-unique prefix -- `gcol_*` on
// `PrismGridColumn`, `gec_*` on `PrismGridEditCell`, `pg_*` on `PrismGrid` -- and every one of
// those exact names was grepped across the ENTIRE repository (not just prism/) before being
// chosen; none collide with any existing struct field anywhere in the codebase. `PrismNode`'s own
// six field names (`kind`/`attrs`/`children`/`key`/`depth`/`count`) are deliberately never reused
// bare here, matching the established rule that the shared foundation type keeps the plain names
// and every other struct works around it.
//
// ── THE CLOSED ENUMS DELIBERATELY AVOID THE BARE TOKEN `None` ─────────────────────────────────
// Both `PrismColumnPin` (start/none/end) and `PrismSelectionMode` (none/single/multi) have a
// "nothing" state, and both name it something other than the literal identifier `None` --
// `Unpinned()` and `SelectionNone()` respectively -- for the exact reason `prism_ui_table.nova`'s
// `PrismSortDirection` avoided it for its own third state (`Unsorted()`, not `None()`): NOVA's
// builtin `Option` type already owns `Some`/`None`, and relying on unverified type-directed
// disambiguation between two different enums that both declare a bare `None` variant is an
// avoidable risk for zero benefit -- the STRING form (returned by `prism_grid_pin_name` /
// `prism_grid_selection_mode_name`) is still exactly "none", matching the task's own vocabulary.
//
// ── A FOURTH TRAP, FOUND BUILDING THIS FILE: LLVM SYMBOLS ARE FLAT, NOT MODULE-QUALIFIED ─────
// The constructor below is named `prism_data_grid`, NOT `prism_grid` -- deliberately different
// from the naming convention `prism_ui_table.nova` uses (its constructor IS named `prism_table`,
// matching its type). A first draft of this file named the constructor `prism_grid` (matching
// `PrismGrid`, exactly the table file's pattern) on the assumption that NOVA's module-qualified
// call syntax (`prism_ui_grid.prism_grid(...)` vs `prism_structure.prism_grid(...)`) would keep
// the two apart, the same way two Java classes in different packages can share a method name.
// It does not: verified directly when linking this file's own KAT -- clang rejected the emitted
// IR with `error: invalid redefinition of function 'prism_grid'`, because NOVA compiles every
// function to a FLAT, UNMANGLED LLVM symbol (`@prism_grid`), and this file already imports
// `prism_structure`, which declares a function of that exact name (the `grid` structural
// primitive body). `_proc_util.ps1`'s own comment on Prism's install step says this outright --
// "folders are for humans, prefixes are for the linker" -- but it is easy to read that as being
// about FILE names only; it applies with equal force to every function name, including a
// constructor whose type-based name looks locally unambiguous. Renamed to `prism_data_grid` to
// resolve it, and noted here so the next component in this tree does not repeat the assumption.
// ── THREE VERIFIED NOVA CALL-SITE TRAPS THIS FILE ROUTES AROUND (NOVA_LANGUAGE_FEATURES.md §7) ─
//   Trap 11 (constructors are file-local): `PrismGridColumn(...)`, `PrismGridEditCell(...)` and
//     `PrismGrid(...)` positional constructors are called bare ONLY inside this file (legal --
//     trap 11 only blocks CROSS-module bare construction); every other module gets them through
//     the `prism_grid_column` / `prism_grid_edit_cell` / `prism_data_grid` wrapper functions below.
//   Trap 13 (`obj.field(args)` on an un-inferred receiver emits an undefined `@nova_rt_<field>`):
//     every call through a column's stored `extract_fn` or a grid's stored `row_key_fn` binds the
//     field to a local FIRST (`let extract_fn = col.gcol_extract` / `let row_key_fn = grid.pg_row_key`),
//     then calls the local -- never `col.gcol_extract(row)` directly, mirroring
//     `prism_ui_table.nova`'s identical fix for the identical shape.
//   Call-site formatting: every function call below keeps its FULL argument list on one source
//     line (however long), matching every existing prism/ file -- NOVA's indentation-significant
//     parser has no verified precedent for a call continued across lines in this codebase, so this
//     file does not attempt to be the first.
//
// PUBLIC API
//   Column pin (closed, 3 states)
//     PrismColumnPin                                        PinnedStart() | Unpinned() | PinnedEnd()
//     prism_grid_pin_start() / _none() / _end()             cross-module ctors
//     prism_grid_pin_name(pin) -> string           exhaustive: "start" | "none" | "end"
//   Selection mode (closed, 3 states)
//     PrismSelectionMode                                    SelectionNone() | SelectionSingle() | SelectionMulti()
//     prism_grid_selection_mode_none() / _single() / _multi()   cross-module ctors
//     prism_grid_selection_mode_name(mode) -> string   exhaustive: "none" | "single" | "multi"
//   Column
//     PrismGridColumn { gcol_key, gcol_header, gcol_extract: any, gcol_pin, gcol_width, gcol_editable }
//     prism_grid_column(key, header, extract_fn, pin, width: int, editable: bool) -> Result<PrismGridColumn>
//       rejects: empty key, empty header, an obviously-non-callable extract_fn, width <= 0
//     prism_grid_column_key/_header(col) -> string   _pin(col) -> string   _width(col) -> int
//     prism_grid_column_editable(col) -> bool
//   Cell-edit target (typed optional payload)
//     PrismGridEditCell { gec_row_key, gec_col_key: string }
//     prism_grid_edit_cell(row_key, col_key) -> PrismGridEditCell     cross-module ctor
//     prism_grid_edit_cell_row_key(e) / _col_key(e) -> string
//   Grid
//     PrismGrid { pg_columns, pg_rows, pg_row_key: any, pg_selection_mode, pg_selected_keys,
//                 pg_editing: PrismGridEditCell? }
//     prism_data_grid(columns: list, rows: list, row_key_fn, selection_mode) -> Result<PrismGrid>
//       rejects: 0 columns, a non-PrismGridColumn element, a duplicate column key, an
//       obviously-non-callable row_key_fn, and "all (or all-but-none) columns pinned" (see header)
//       -- stores `columns` in the deterministic pin-grouped order (see header), not input order.
//     prism_grid_columns(grid) -> list      (pin-grouped order)     _rows(grid) -> list
//     prism_grid_row_count(grid) -> int
//     prism_grid_selection_mode(grid) -> string        _selected_keys(grid) -> list
//     prism_grid_editing_cell(grid) -> PrismGridEditCell?
//   Row selection
//     prism_grid_select_row(grid, key: string) -> Result<PrismGrid>
//       `none` mode: always rejected. `single`: REPLACES the selection. `multi`: ACCUMULATES
//       (re-selecting an already-selected key is a no-op, not a duplicate entry). Rejects a key
//       naming no row. Non-mutating.
//   Cell edit state
//     prism_grid_begin_edit(grid, row_key: string, col_key: string) -> Result<PrismGrid>
//       rejects an unknown column, a non-editable column, or an unknown row. Non-mutating.
//     prism_grid_end_edit(grid) -> PrismGrid    (cannot fail -- clears edit state)
//   Column resize
//     prism_grid_resize_column(grid, key: string, new_width: int) -> Result<PrismGrid>
//       rejects new_width <= 0 or an unknown column key. Non-mutating.
//   Column reorder
//     prism_grid_reorder(grid, key: string, to_index: int) -> Result<PrismGrid>
//       rejects an unknown key, an out-of-range to_index, or a to_index that would move the
//       column OUT of its own pin group (never silently clamped). Non-mutating.
//   Rendering
//     prism_grid_render(grid) -> Result<PrismNode>
//       header band of column headers, stacked above the body built via
//       `prism_structure.prism_grid` (mandatory row-identity threaded through unmodified, exactly
//       as `prism_ui_table.nova` does). Every body cell carries "selected" and "editing" bool
//       attrs (always present, never merely absent-means-false) marking whether that cell's row
//       is in the current selection and whether that exact (row, column) is the active edit target.

import prism_node
import prism_arrange
import prism_content
import prism_structure
import prism_ui_kit

// ── Column pin: closed, 3 states (see header) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
enum PrismColumnPin
    PinnedStart()
    Unpinned()
    PinnedEnd()

fn prism_grid_pin_start() -> PrismColumnPin
    PinnedStart()
fn prism_grid_pin_none() -> PrismColumnPin
    Unpinned()
fn prism_grid_pin_end() -> PrismColumnPin
    PinnedEnd()

// Exhaustive, no wildcard arm -- same discipline PrismSortDirection/PrismNodeKind follow.
fn prism_grid_pin_name(pin: PrismColumnPin) -> string
    match pin
        PinnedStart() => "start"
        Unpinned() => "none"
        PinnedEnd() => "end"

// ── Selection mode: closed, 3 states (see header) ────────────────────────────────────────────
enum PrismSelectionMode
    SelectionNone()
    SelectionSingle()
    SelectionMulti()

fn prism_grid_selection_mode_none() -> PrismSelectionMode
    SelectionNone()
fn prism_grid_selection_mode_single() -> PrismSelectionMode
    SelectionSingle()
fn prism_grid_selection_mode_multi() -> PrismSelectionMode
    SelectionMulti()

fn prism_grid_selection_mode_name(mode: PrismSelectionMode) -> string
    match mode
        SelectionNone() => "none"
        SelectionSingle() => "single"
        SelectionMulti() => "multi"

// ── Column ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

type PrismGridColumn
    gcol_key: string
    gcol_header: string
    gcol_extract: any
    gcol_pin: PrismColumnPin
    gcol_width: int
    gcol_editable: bool

// `extract_fn` is stored, never called here -- same coarse "obviously not callable" guard
// `prism_ui_table.nova`'s `prism_table_column` uses, for the identical reason (no `is_fn`
// introspection exists; a real closure never reports true from these coarse kind checks).
fn prism_grid_column(key: string, header: string, extract_fn, pin: PrismColumnPin, width: int, editable: bool) -> Result<PrismGridColumn>
    if key == ""
        return err("prism_grid_column: key must not be empty")
    if header == ""
        return err("prism_grid_column: header must not be empty")
    if is_string(extract_fn) or is_int(extract_fn) or is_float(extract_fn) or is_bool(extract_fn) or is_list(extract_fn) or is_dict(extract_fn)
        return err("prism_grid_column: extract_fn must be a callback fn(row) -> string, got a " + type_of(extract_fn))
    if width <= 0
        return err("prism_grid_column: width must be > 0, got " + str(width))
    ok(PrismGridColumn(key, header, extract_fn, pin, width, editable))

fn prism_grid_column_key(col: PrismGridColumn) -> string
    col.gcol_key

fn prism_grid_column_header(col: PrismGridColumn) -> string
    col.gcol_header

fn prism_grid_column_pin(col: PrismGridColumn) -> string
    prism_grid_pin_name(col.gcol_pin)

fn prism_grid_column_width(col: PrismGridColumn) -> int
    col.gcol_width

fn prism_grid_column_editable(col: PrismGridColumn) -> bool
    col.gcol_editable

// Rebuilds a column with a NEW width, everything else unchanged -- an internal helper for
// `prism_grid_resize_column`, which has already validated `new_width > 0` before calling this, so
// no re-validation happens here (this is not the public constructor).
fn _prism_grid_column_resized(col: PrismGridColumn, new_width: int) -> PrismGridColumn
    PrismGridColumn(col.gcol_key, col.gcol_header, col.gcol_extract, col.gcol_pin, new_width, col.gcol_editable)

// ── Cell-edit target ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

type PrismGridEditCell
    gec_row_key: string
    gec_col_key: string

fn prism_grid_edit_cell(row_key: string, col_key: string) -> PrismGridEditCell
    PrismGridEditCell(row_key, col_key)

fn prism_grid_edit_cell_row_key(e: PrismGridEditCell) -> string
    e.gec_row_key

fn prism_grid_edit_cell_col_key(e: PrismGridEditCell) -> string
    e.gec_col_key

// ── Grid ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

type PrismGrid
    pg_columns: list<PrismGridColumn>
    pg_rows: list
    pg_row_key: any
    pg_selection_mode: PrismSelectionMode
    pg_selected_keys: list
    pg_editing: PrismGridEditCell?

fn _prism_grid_is_start_pinned(pin: PrismColumnPin) -> bool
    match pin
        PinnedStart() => true
        Unpinned() => false
        PinnedEnd() => false

fn _prism_grid_is_end_pinned(pin: PrismColumnPin) -> bool
    match pin
        PinnedStart() => false
        Unpinned() => false
        PinnedEnd() => true

fn _prism_grid_count_start_pinned(columns: list) -> int
    let mut n = 0
    for c in columns
        if _prism_grid_is_start_pinned(c.gcol_pin)
            n += 1
    n

fn _prism_grid_count_end_pinned(columns: list) -> int
    let mut n = 0
    for c in columns
        if _prism_grid_is_end_pinned(c.gcol_pin)
            n += 1
    n

// Stable 3-way partition: start-pinned (declared order) ++ unpinned (declared order) ++
// end-pinned (declared order) -- see header for why this makes the ordering deterministic AND
// total, and why it is computed exactly once (at construction) rather than at every read.
fn _prism_grid_canonical_order(columns: list) -> list
    let start_cols = []
    let mid_cols = []
    let end_cols = []
    for c in columns
        match c.gcol_pin
            PinnedStart() => start_cols.push(c)
            Unpinned() => mid_cols.push(c)
            PinnedEnd() => end_cols.push(c)
    let out = []
    for c in start_cols
        out.push(c)
    for c in mid_cols
        out.push(c)
    for c in end_cols
        out.push(c)
    out

// The one chokepoint every column-key lookup funnels through (mirrors `prism_ui_table.nova`'s
// `_prism_table_find_column`).
fn _prism_grid_find_column(columns: list, key: string) -> Result<PrismGridColumn>
    for c in columns
        if c.gcol_key == key
            return ok(c)
    err("prism_grid: unknown column key '" + key + "'")

fn _prism_grid_list_contains(xs: list, v: string) -> bool
    for x in xs
        if x == v
            return true
    false

// Computes every row's identity key EXACTLY ONCE per call, validating the untyped row_key_fn's
// return is actually a string before it is used (same guard class as `prism_ui_table.nova`'s
// `_prism_table_build_pairs`). Used by selection/edit validation to answer "does this key name a
// real row" without duplicating the guard at each call site.
fn _prism_grid_row_keys(grid: PrismGrid) -> Result<list>
    let row_key_fn = grid.pg_row_key
    let out = []
    let mut idx = 0
    for r in grid.pg_rows
        let k = row_key_fn(r)
        if not is_string(k)
            return err("prism_grid: row_key_fn must return a string, row " + str(idx) + " returned " + type_name(k))
        out.push(k)
        idx += 1
    ok(out)

// Rejects: 0 columns, a non-PrismGridColumn element (named by index and actual type), a duplicate
// column key, an obviously-non-callable row_key_fn (stored, never called here), and the
// all-pinned invariant (see header: count(start)+count(end) >= total is rejected, naming both
// counts). Stores `columns` in the deterministic pin-grouped order, not the caller's input order.
fn prism_data_grid(columns: list, rows: list, row_key_fn, selection_mode: PrismSelectionMode) -> Result<PrismGrid>
    if len(columns) == 0
        return err("prism_grid: needs at least one column")
    if is_string(row_key_fn) or is_int(row_key_fn) or is_float(row_key_fn) or is_bool(row_key_fn) or is_list(row_key_fn) or is_dict(row_key_fn)
        return err("prism_grid: row_key_fn must be a callback fn(row) -> string, got a " + type_of(row_key_fn))
    let seen = {}
    let mut idx = 0
    for c in columns
        if type_name(c) != "PrismGridColumn"
            return err("prism_grid: column " + str(idx) + " is not a PrismGridColumn (got " + type_name(c) + ")")
        if c.gcol_key in seen
            return err("prism_grid: duplicate column key '" + c.gcol_key + "'")
        seen[c.gcol_key] = true
        idx += 1
    let n = len(columns)
    let count_start = _prism_grid_count_start_pinned(columns)
    let count_end = _prism_grid_count_end_pinned(columns)
    if count_start + count_end >= n
        return err("prism_grid: " + str(count_start + count_end) + " of " + str(n) + " column(s) are pinned -- at least one column must remain unpinned (a grid with no scrollable columns is not usable)")
    let ordered = _prism_grid_canonical_order(columns)
    ok(PrismGrid(ordered, rows, row_key_fn, selection_mode, [], none()))

fn prism_grid_columns(grid: PrismGrid) -> list
    grid.pg_columns

fn prism_grid_rows(grid: PrismGrid) -> list
    grid.pg_rows

fn prism_grid_row_count(grid: PrismGrid) -> int
    len(grid.pg_rows)

fn prism_grid_selection_mode(grid: PrismGrid) -> string
    prism_grid_selection_mode_name(grid.pg_selection_mode)

fn prism_grid_selected_keys(grid: PrismGrid) -> list
    grid.pg_selected_keys

fn prism_grid_editing_cell(grid: PrismGrid) -> PrismGridEditCell?
    grid.pg_editing

// ── Row selection ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `none`: always rejected. `single`: REPLACES the current selection with `[key]`. `multi`:
// ACCUMULATES -- appends `key` unless it is already selected (idempotent, never a duplicate
// entry). Both `single` and `multi` reject a key naming no row (checked via `_prism_grid_row_keys`,
// which itself guards the untyped row_key_fn's return). Non-mutating: every branch builds a FRESH
// `PrismGrid` value; `grid` itself is never altered.
fn prism_grid_select_row(grid: PrismGrid, key: string) -> Result<PrismGrid>
    match grid.pg_selection_mode
        SelectionNone() =>
            err("prism_grid_select_row: selection is disabled (mode = none)")
        SelectionSingle() =>
            let keys = _prism_grid_row_keys(grid)?
            if not _prism_grid_list_contains(keys, key)
                return err("prism_grid_select_row: unknown row key '" + key + "'")
            ok(PrismGrid(grid.pg_columns, grid.pg_rows, grid.pg_row_key, grid.pg_selection_mode, [key], grid.pg_editing))
        SelectionMulti() =>
            let keys2 = _prism_grid_row_keys(grid)?
            if not _prism_grid_list_contains(keys2, key)
                return err("prism_grid_select_row: unknown row key '" + key + "'")
            if _prism_grid_list_contains(grid.pg_selected_keys, key)
                return ok(grid)
            let new_keys = []
            for k in grid.pg_selected_keys
                new_keys.push(k)
            new_keys.push(key)
            ok(PrismGrid(grid.pg_columns, grid.pg_rows, grid.pg_row_key, grid.pg_selection_mode, new_keys, grid.pg_editing))

// ── Cell edit state ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Rejects, in order: an unknown column key, a column marked non-editable, an unknown row key.
// Non-mutating.
fn prism_grid_begin_edit(grid: PrismGrid, row_key: string, col_key: string) -> Result<PrismGrid>
    let col = _prism_grid_find_column(grid.pg_columns, col_key)?
    if not col.gcol_editable
        return err("prism_grid_begin_edit: column '" + col_key + "' is not editable")
    let keys = _prism_grid_row_keys(grid)?
    if not _prism_grid_list_contains(keys, row_key)
        return err("prism_grid_begin_edit: unknown row key '" + row_key + "'")
    ok(PrismGrid(grid.pg_columns, grid.pg_rows, grid.pg_row_key, grid.pg_selection_mode, grid.pg_selected_keys, some(prism_grid_edit_cell(row_key, col_key))))

// Cannot fail: clearing the edit target is always legal, whether or not one was set.
fn prism_grid_end_edit(grid: PrismGrid) -> PrismGrid
    PrismGrid(grid.pg_columns, grid.pg_rows, grid.pg_row_key, grid.pg_selection_mode, grid.pg_selected_keys, none())

// ── Column resize ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Rejects new_width <= 0 or an unknown column key. Non-mutating: every other column is copied
// through unchanged; only the matched column is rebuilt (via `_prism_grid_column_resized`).
fn prism_grid_resize_column(grid: PrismGrid, key: string, new_width: int) -> Result<PrismGrid>
    if new_width <= 0
        return err("prism_grid_resize_column: width must be > 0, got " + str(new_width))
    let mut found = false
    let new_cols = []
    for c in grid.pg_columns
        if c.gcol_key == key
            found = true
            new_cols.push(_prism_grid_column_resized(c, new_width))
        else
            new_cols.push(c)
    if not found
        return err("prism_grid_resize_column: unknown column key '" + key + "'")
    ok(PrismGrid(new_cols, grid.pg_rows, grid.pg_row_key, grid.pg_selection_mode, grid.pg_selected_keys, grid.pg_editing))

// ── Column reorder ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The valid `[lo, hi)` index range for a column carrying `pin`, given the grid's pin-group sizes
// -- see header: because `pg_columns` is ALWAYS stored start-block-then-mid-block-then-end-block,
// each group is a single contiguous range, so this is a pure arithmetic lookup, not a re-scan.
fn _prism_grid_pin_range(pin: PrismColumnPin, count_start: int, count_end: int, n: int) -> list
    match pin
        PinnedStart() => [0, count_start]
        Unpinned() => [count_start, n - count_end]
        PinnedEnd() => [n - count_end, n]

// Moves the element at `from_index` to end up at `to_index` in the returned list (both indices
// into the FINAL n-length list). O(n): remove, then re-insert while walking the shortened list.
fn _prism_grid_move_column(cols: list, from_index: int, to_index: int) -> list
    let moved = cols[from_index]
    let without = []
    let mut i = 0
    for c in cols
        if i != from_index
            without.push(c)
        i += 1
    let result = []
    let mut inserted = false
    let mut j = 0
    for c in without
        if j == to_index
            result.push(moved)
            inserted = true
        result.push(c)
        j += 1
    if not inserted
        result.push(moved)
    result

// Rejects, in order: an unknown column key, an out-of-range `to_index`, and a `to_index` that
// falls outside the column's OWN pin group's contiguous range -- the last case explicitly named
// ("would cross pin groups"), never silently clamped into range. Non-mutating: builds a fresh
// column list via `_prism_grid_move_column`; `grid.pg_columns` itself is untouched.
fn prism_grid_reorder(grid: PrismGrid, key: string, to_index: int) -> Result<PrismGrid>
    let cols = grid.pg_columns
    let n = len(cols)
    let mut from_index = -1
    let mut idx = 0
    for c in cols
        if c.gcol_key == key
            from_index = idx
        idx += 1
    if from_index < 0
        return err("prism_grid_reorder: unknown column key '" + key + "'")
    if to_index < 0 or to_index >= n
        return err("prism_grid_reorder: to_index " + str(to_index) + " is out of range for " + str(n) + " column(s)")
    let found = cols[from_index]
    let count_start = _prism_grid_count_start_pinned(cols)
    let count_end = _prism_grid_count_end_pinned(cols)
    let range = _prism_grid_pin_range(found.gcol_pin, count_start, count_end, n)
    let lo = range[0]
    let hi = range[1]
    if to_index < lo or to_index >= hi
        return err("prism_grid_reorder: column '" + key + "' is " + prism_grid_pin_name(found.gcol_pin) + "-pinned -- cannot move it to index " + str(to_index) + ", outside its pin group's range [" + str(lo) + ", " + str(hi) + ")")
    let new_cols = _prism_grid_move_column(cols, from_index, to_index)
    ok(PrismGrid(new_cols, grid.pg_rows, grid.pg_row_key, grid.pg_selection_mode, grid.pg_selected_keys, grid.pg_editing))

// ── Rendering ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn _prism_grid_header_texts(grid: PrismGrid) -> list
    let out = []
    for c in grid.pg_columns
        out.push(c.gcol_header)
    out

fn _prism_grid_column_keys(grid: PrismGrid) -> list
    let out = []
    for c in grid.pg_columns
        out.push(c.gcol_key)
    out

fn _prism_grid_header_band(grid: PrismGrid) -> Result<PrismNode>
    let texts = _prism_grid_header_texts(grid)
    let labels = prism_ui_kit.prism_ui_labels(texts)?
    prism_arrange.prism_band(labels)

// One body cell: resolves the column, extracts display text (validated to actually be a string,
// same guard class as `prism_ui_table.nova`'s `_prism_table_cell`), then marks the cell with two
// ALWAYS-PRESENT bool attrs -- "selected" (this cell's row is in the current selection) and
// "editing" (this exact row+column is the active edit target) -- via the same
// build-then-dict_merge-then-reconstruct technique `prism_structure.nova`'s `prism_tabs` uses for
// its "tab_label" attr. `col.gcol_extract` and `grid.pg_row_key` are both bound to a LOCAL before
// being called (never `col.gcol_extract(row)` directly) -- see header, NOVA_LANGUAGE_FEATURES.md
// trap 13.
fn _prism_grid_cell(grid: PrismGrid, row, col_key: string) -> Result<PrismNode>
    let col = _prism_grid_find_column(grid.pg_columns, col_key)?
    let extract_fn = col.gcol_extract
    let text = extract_fn(row)
    if not is_string(text)
        return err("prism_grid: column '" + col_key + "' extract_fn must return a string, got " + type_name(text))
    let base = prism_content.prism_label(text)?
    let row_key_fn = grid.pg_row_key
    let rkey = row_key_fn(row)
    let is_selected = _prism_grid_list_contains(grid.pg_selected_keys, rkey)
    let is_editing = match grid.pg_editing
        Some(ec) => ec.gec_row_key == rkey and ec.gec_col_key == col_key
        None => false
    let merged = dict_merge(base.attrs, {"selected": is_selected, "editing": is_editing})
    prism_node.prism_node_new(base.kind, merged, base.children, base.key)

// Composes: header band of column headers (in the deterministic pin-grouped order), then the
// body via `prism_structure.prism_grid` -- `grid.pg_row_key` threaded through unmodified as the
// mandatory row-identity selector, exactly as `prism_ui_table.nova`'s `prism_table_render` does,
// so a duplicate row identity is rejected exactly where that primitive already rejects it.
fn prism_grid_render(grid: PrismGrid) -> Result<PrismNode>
    let header_node = _prism_grid_header_band(grid)?
    let col_keys = _prism_grid_column_keys(grid)
    let body_node = prism_structure.prism_grid(grid.pg_rows, grid.pg_row_key, col_keys, fn(row, col_key) _prism_grid_cell(grid, row, col_key))?
    prism_arrange.prism_stack([header_node, body_node])
